course: dictionary
lesson: 1531
title: Colour words
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Cerise is a colour word. It means a bright, deep pinkish-red, like cherries. When you meet it, look at the words around it, because they are its context and they give you clues. If a painting shows a cerise dress, you know the dress is brighter and pinker than plain red.
explain: Cerise came into English from French, where cerise means cherry. That is why the colour is linked to cherries. To check your meaning, put it in the sentence: «The cerise flowers glowed in the sun» still makes sense if you think of bright pinkish-red flowers.
explain: Cerise can be an adjective, which describes a noun, as in «a cerise scarf». It can also be a noun for the colour itself, as in «the cerise was bright». Notice that the noun is the colour, not the fruit.
fact: Cerise means a bright, deep pinkish-red colour, like cherries.
fact: Cerise comes from the French word for cherry.
fact: Cerise can be an adjective (a cerise scarf) or a noun for the colour (the cerise is bright).
fact: In the counting rhyme «Un, deux, trois, nous irons au bois», the French word cerises means cherries.
fact: A good check is to swap your meaning into the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
exercise: What does cerise mean? | A bright yellow colour, like a lemon | A pale grey colour, like a cloud | A dark blue colour, like the night sky | *A bright, deep pinkish-red colour, like cherries
exercise: «Mia painted the sky cerise at sunset.» What does cerise tell the reader? | That the sun was cold | That the sky was dark and stormy | That Mia was hungry | *The exact shade: a bright, deep pinkish-red
exercise: Which word in the rhyme «Un, deux, trois, nous irons au bois» means cherries? | *cerises | trois | nous | deux
test: «Her cerise dress stood out in the crowd.» Which word tells you the colour? | dress | *cerise | crowd | stood
test: Ben says cerise is just plain red. Is he right? | Yes, cerise and plain red mean exactly the same | *No, cerise is brighter and deeper, a pinkish-red like cherries | Yes, but only in a painting | No, cerise is a pale grey
exam: Which sentence uses cerise correctly? | *The cerise flowers glowed in the garden. | She ate a cerise sandwich for lunch. | The cerise river froze solid overnight. | The cerise wind blew all day.
